We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x, Dv(x)\right)dx\,:\, v\in
  \mathcal{K}_\psi(\Omega)\right\}.
  \end{equation*} Given the growth restrictions imposed on the function $f$, with respect to the gradient variable, we find that when the obstacle function is bounded and satisfies certain regularity conditions, the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ being part of the Sobolev space, leads to a corresponding increase in the differentiability of the solution, provided the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ belongs to a $W^{1, p+2}$. Notably, we address subquadratic growth conditions on the function $f$, i.e., where $f(x,\xi)\approx a(x)|\xi|^p$ and $1<p<2$ and map $a$ also belongs to a Sobolev space, where $\Omega$ and $x,\xi\in \Omega$. Furthermore, we demonstrate that after transforming the problem to a non-degenerate elliptic PDE, the corresponding partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ being part of the Sobolev space, leads to a corresponding boost in the differentiability of the solution, under the conditions mentioned above. This research builds upon the work presented in the paper "Higher differentiability properties of solutions to the obstacle problem" by M. Szumalski and F. J. Su\'ez, where the case of autonomous functions $f$ was examined. When the obstacle function satisfies certain regularity conditions and the growth restrictions imposed on the function $f$, with respect to the gradient variable, where $f(x,\xi)\approx a(x)|\xi|^p$ and $1<p<2$ and map $a$ also belongs to a Sobolev space, where $\Omega$ and $x,\xi\in \Omega$, it is demonstrated that the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ being part of the Sobolev space, leads to a corresponding increase in the differentiability of the solution, provided the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ belongs to a $W^{
